faq:
  - q: "Should I tailor my resume for every job?"
    a: "For jobs you actually want, yes. Not a complete rewrite — targeted keyword optimization and summary adjustment. The tailoring that matters most: keywords in Skills and summary, and the order/prominence of bullets within each role. A targeted resume converts at 2–4x the rate of a generic one."
  - q: "How long should resume tailoring take?"
    a: "15–30 minutes per application once you have a strong base resume. Most of the work is: copy in the new job description, check keyword gaps, update Skills section, adjust summary. The base resume stays 90% the same — you are adjusting the top 10% for each application."
  - q: "What parts of a resume should be tailored?"
    a: "Three parts: (1) Professional summary — match the target title and key requirements. (2) Skills section — include exact keywords from the job posting. (3) Bullet order — front-load bullets most relevant to this specific role. Do not rewrite every bullet for every job — this is inefficient and rarely worth it."
  - q: "What is keyword mirroring in a resume?"
    a: "Using the exact language from the job description in your resume — not a synonym, not a paraphrase. If the job says 'Python,' your resume says 'Python.' If it says 'cross-functional stakeholder management,' mirror that phrase. ATS systems parse for exact and near-exact matches. Synonyms are treated as misses."
  - q: "Can you tailor a resume too much?"
    a: "Yes — if you change so much that the resume no longer reflects your actual experience accurately, or if you claim skills you do not have. Tailoring means surfacing and prioritizing relevant experience, not fabricating it."
howto:
